  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure remains an influence through weekend as it remains\nparked nearby or overhead leading to drier conditions for most\nthis weekend. A series of weak disturbances are projected to \nmove through each afternoon leading to chances for isolated \nshowers and thunderstorms across the northeastern mountains this\nafternoon and more scattered activity on Sunday. Severe weather\nis not expected, but an isolated strong storm cannot be ruled \nout, especially along the upslope side of the mountains.\n\nTemperatures will continue on a warming trend through the \nweekend with mid to upper 80s this afternoon and Sunday\nafternoon across the usual warm spots in the lowlands.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nShortwave disturbances are still slated to cross the area late\nMonday and Tuesday leading to an unsettled weather pattern\nemerging as we start the new week. Most of Monday will be dry,\noutside of the chance for a decaying MCS that some models show\nmoving across Kentucky and southern West Virginia early in the\nmorning. Whether or not this affects the secondary activation of\nconvection remains unknown, but CAPE is projected to recover to\nabout 1,000-2,000 J/Kg in the afternoon. The latest NAM run has\nscattered showers and thunderstorms reforming late in the\nafternoon and through the evening. Some storms may be strong to\nsevere, mostly across our portions of the Ohio River Valley as \nupper-level support from a jet enters the area. Still many\ndetails that need to be ironed out once hi-res guidance gets in\nrange. \n\nFlash flooding looks to be an issue with this activity late\nMonday with rounds of heavy rainfall, but particularly on \nTuesday, as PWATs between 1.50\" and 2.25\" move over the area. \nThe NAM currently shows a swath of an inch to an inch and a half\nof rainfall being picked up across our Kentucky counties and \nfar northern West Virginia.\n\nA drier spell looks to arrive on Wednesday behind this active \npattern, spanning the rest of the week as high pressure\ncontinues to settle in. Still, isolated chances for afternoon\nstorms will exist due to peak heating and a 'dirty ridge' \nsetting up.",
